DTSTAMP:20261009T184050Z
UID:stanford_campus-localist-54171323569878-54171323569879@local-events
DESCRIPTION:Abstract\n\nBatteries are increasingly central to transportati
 on and the electric grid\, yet many of the processes that determine their 
 lifetime\, safety\, and performance remain hidden from conventional measur
 ements. In this talk\, I will discuss two complementary approaches for gai
 ning access to this hidden information. First\, cryogenic electron microsc
 opy enables direct visualization of battery interfaces and degradation pro
 cesses at the nanoscale. Second\, machine-learning-based virtual sensing c
 an\n\nAges: All ages\nLast synced: 2026-10-09 11:40 PDT
LOCATION:Green Earth Sciences Building
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:[Department of Art & Art History / Stanford Arts Institute] Tangli
 ng the Digital: Weaving\, Printmaking\, Computing
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261012T130000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261012T170000
DTSTAMP:20261009T184050Z
UID:stanford_campus-localist-53948654465100-53948654480470@local-events
DESCRIPTION:Digital objects are often seen as alien to craft\, as though n
 o hands had shaped them. They can be copied endlessly and exactly\, bereft
  of the maker’s touch. Yet weaving and printmaking are among our oldest 
 technologies of reproduction\, fixing patterns so they can be repeated and
  circulated. The printing press and the Jacquard loom initiated processes 
 of automation that displaced human hands—and that still animate our digi
 tal tools.\n\nWarp and weft describe not just a textile structure but a lo
 gic\n\nAges: All ages\nLast synced: 2026-10-09 11:40 PDT
LOCATION:McMurtry Building
END:VEVENT
